WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:04.870
Netflix might have a lot to offer, but critics
aren't crazy about all of their originals.

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:08.950
Well, we beg to differ - in fact, there's
a lot to love when it comes to some of the

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:13.700
streaming services less-than-critically acclaimed
fair. Here's a reappraisal of the Netflix

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.220
series that critics were totally wrong about.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:21.820
Vanity Fair posited that Girlboss might have
been Netflix's first "truly terrible show":

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:26.859
a smug summation of millenials at their worst,
embodied by supposed heroine and wannabe mogul

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:33.060
Sophia Amoruso. It didn't help that the real-life
Amoruso, CEO of online fashion retailer Nasty

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:38.090
Gal, was catching flak for her business practices
as the show premiered. Girlboss wasn't just

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:41.500
bad, it was badly timed as well - according
to critics, at least.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:47.340
"Come on, Sophia. You can do this. You do
not want a bad review. A bad review is death."

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.040
But the show, on its own, is a whip-smart
delight. Critics slammed the fictionalized

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:56.480
Amoruso for being imperfect, accusing her
character of being vapid and narcissistic.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:01.360
The reality is simpler: this is a show about
a person being as messy and fallible as people

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:05.890
often are. Audiences don't care if Amoruso
makes mistakes - in fact, that's why they're

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:10.390
watching. We love it in Don Draper, we love
it in Walter White, and we love it in Sophia

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:15.190
Amoruso. She's a brat? Sure. She's ruthless?
Bring it on. We're not here for a lesson - we're

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:19.120
here for a story, and Girlboss delivers with
drama and style to spare.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.800
"Oh! Did I just detect the tiniest gleam of
interest on your face?"

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:29.120
This impassioned biopic chronicles the controversial
life and death of Madalyn Murray O'Hair, the

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.450
founder of American Atheists and the leader
of the push to take prayer out of public schools.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.880
"Well, you'd better start a-g-prayin',
'cause we're gonna take this all the way to

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.400
the Supreme Court if we have to."

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:44.520
Critics called it disjointed and unfocused,
claiming that it ping-ponged crazily between

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.799
comedy, tragedy, and points in between. This
isn't entirely untrue: The Most Hated Woman

00:01:49.799 --> 00:01:55.310
in America plays fast and loose with the audience's
expectations. But the reality is that O'Hair's

00:01:55.310 --> 00:02:00.189
life, which included legal victories, family
drama, and an untimely and violent death,

00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:02.439
was just as bizarre and prone to wild shifts.

00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:07.369
Anchored by Melissa Leo's stirring performance,
it is a film as utterly unique as its subject,

00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:11.920
a journey from deep love to towering pride
and all that comes from the collision of those

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.840
two fundamental forces. O'Hair resists categorization:
she is neither the triumphant crusader nor

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.510
the sneering witch. This movie honors that,
and chooses to live in the volatile in-between

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:28.180
where she can be the person she was: frustrating,
contradictory, and driven by unshakable conviction.

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:34.440
Chloë Grace Moretz plays Susannah Cahalan
in this terrifying descent into medical turmoil,

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:40.060
based on the real-life Cahalan's battle with
anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis . Her symptoms

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:45.209
are harrowing and sudden, sending her into
periods of rage, paranoia, and extreme sensitivity

00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:50.130
that seem to have no cause. Doctors suggest
all manner of incorrect diagnoses as Cahalan

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:56.220
sinks further and further into abject suffering,
grabbing desperately at remedies for exhaustion,

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.220
schizophrenia, and sleep deprivation.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:05.520
"You gotta get me outta here. I don't belong
here. And they're all saying bad stuff about

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.020
me."

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.480
Critics claimed the movie relied too heavily
on clichés… which means they're probably

00:03:10.489 --> 00:03:15.980
ignoring the fact that Cahalan's story really
did end with an eleventh-hour deus ex machina:

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:20.700
the moment the right doctor identified the
illness ravaging her brain and the relatively

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.140
straightforward road to recovery it led her
on.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:29.060
"You're going to be just fine. I've found
you."

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:34.160
Beyond this, however, is Moretz's underrated
performance, which veers wildly as her character's

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.420
brain careens more and more off-track.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:40.760
Brain on Fire ends with an unlikely victory,
but it's first and foremost a story about

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:45.450
illness: the way it can destabilize a life,
the way it launches sufferers into a strange

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:50.209
and scary new world of uncertainty, and the
way it can be solved by the unlikeliest confluence

00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:50.700
of fate.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:56.540
This adaptation of L. M. Montgomery's beloved
Anne of Green Gables series has proved divisive

00:03:56.550 --> 00:04:01.690
to say the least. Longtime fans and critics
alike condemn its darkness; rather than leaving

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:05.879
Anne's traumatic childhood behind, it examines
how her experiences might have shaped her

00:04:05.879 --> 00:04:10.430
in the form of flashbacks and panic attacks.
Moreover, characters like Gilbert Blythe,

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:14.690
Anne's love interest, and Matthew and Marilla,
the brother and sister who become parents

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:19.630
to Anne, are given entirely original subplots
of their own. Critics argue that these additions

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:25.650
are unnecessary at best, needlessly grim at
worst. No one returns to Anne Shirley's adventures

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:28.780
for pathos, they argue. They want what they
already love.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:36.860
"You know too well the impact of an untimely
death. Anne has suffered so much loss."

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:40.740
The vast
majority of the series is faithful to Montgomery's

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:45.590
starry-eyed vision: Anne loses herself in
the pleasures of friendship, raspberry cordial,

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:50.310
and teaching other orphans to read. This adaptation
is merely interested in examining the hardships

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:52.000
that make the cheery moments possible.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:57.800
"All we can do is our best, Marilla, regardless
of what we know or don't know."

00:04:58.460 --> 00:04:59.200
"That's true enough."

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.900
This chronicle of a 1930s Iowa farmers' strike
was derided by critics for feeling overlong

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:09.741
and more than a little like homework. While
it might not be everyone's cup of tea, for

00:05:09.741 --> 00:05:14.660
the connoisseur of historical drama, Damnation
is anything but dull. It's a brutal story,

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:19.130
to be sure, and it doesn't offer hope without
reason. In a world crowded with happy-go-lucky

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:24.120
hobos riding trains to Rock Candy Mountain,
there is need for a show as dark in tone as

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:24.660
Damnation.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.070
"You seem to be shooting at your chickens."

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:32.340
"No. I'm shooting at that egg-stealing son
of a b---- that's cowerin' in there with 'em.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:33.840
Can't afford to lose more eggs!"

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:39.400
The writing is strong, the performances passionate,
and the drama is rooted in a real American

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:43.919
struggle that echoes to this day. It is, in
fact, very much in line with the American

00:05:43.919 --> 00:05:48.540
tradition of flinty-eyed worker's stories.
If it's grim, it's because it's telling the

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:52.560
truth, and there is always room for the truth
in our adaptations of the past.

